%I A401173 #14 Nov 02 2026 16:29:06
%S A401173 3,5,37,59,61,109,359,5507,2117573,503734381,20900146669
%N A401173 Primes p such that every element x in GF(p) eventually reaches 1 under iteration of the polynomial map T(x) = (x^2 - 2)^2 mod p.
%C A401173 Also called "Unital Collapse Primes" (or "Observer-Threshold Collapse Primes" in Distinction-Observation Theory, DOT).
%C A401173 Equivalently, primes p such that the backward orbit (preimage tree) of 1 under T(x) = (x^2 - 2)^2 mod p has size p, so the functional graph of T on GF(p) is a single connected component rooted at the fixed point x = 1 (i.e., T has no periodic cycles in GF(p) other than {1}).
%C A401173 Note that x^2 - 2 = 2*T_2(x/2) is the monic Chebyshev polynomial of degree 2 (Lucas-Lehmer map), whereas T(x) = (x^2 - 2)^2 squares this map, breaking the Chebyshev semigroup commutation property and merging the sign orbits {+y, -y}. The backward preimage tree of 1 consists of finite-field evaluations of Ramanujan-type nested radicals +-sqrt(2 +- sqrt(+-sqrt(2 +- ...))).
%C A401173 For p = 2, the basin of 1 has size 1 (since T(0) = 0 and T(1) = 1).
%C A401173 Galois stratification, Hilbert class field of Q(sqrt(-83)), and Ramanujan-Gauss quadratic form:
%C A401173 - If p == 7 or 19 (mod 24), the preimage tree of 1 has exact size 2 (namely {1, p-1}) because -1 and 3 are quadratic non-residues mod p. Thus no prime in this sequence is congruent to 7 or 19 (mod 24).
%C A401173 - For p > 5, the primitive fixed-point cubic Phi_1*(x) = (T(x) - x)/(x - 1) = x^3 + x^2 - 3x - 4 has prime discriminant -83 and generates the Hilbert class field of Q(sqrt(-83)) (class number h(-83) = 3). Consequently, Phi_1*(x) is irreducible mod p if and only if p is represented by the non-principal Ramanujan-Gauss binary quadratic form p = 3*x^2 + x*y + 7*y^2 (equivalently, the p-th Fourier coefficient of the weight-1 modular form eta(tau)*eta(83*tau) equals -1), whereas primes of the principal form p = x^2 + x*y + 21*y^2 split Phi_1*(x) into 3 fixed points and are excluded. Explicit representations p = 3*x^2 + x*y + 7*y^2 for a(3)..a(10): 37 = (3,1), 59 = (4,1), 61 = (3,2), 109 = (-6,1), 359 = (-11,1), 5507 = (-43,5), 2117573 = (209,518), 503734381 = (-13014,1421).
%C A401173 - The primitive period-2 polynomial Phi_2*(x) = (T(T(x)) - x)/(T(x) - x) of degree 12 has discriminant -18111297736205547 = -3 * 641 * 857^2 * 3581^2, with squarefree part -3*641 (where 641 is Euler's factor of the 5th Fermat number F_5 = 2^32 + 1).
%C A401173 - All terms p > 5 belong to the residue classes p == 1, 5, 11, 13, 17, 23 (mod 24). Notably, a(5)=61, a(6)=109, a(9)=2117573, and a(10)=503734381 all lie in the same congruence class p == 13 (mod 24) and p == 61 (mod 120).
%C A401173 Every point c_n = T^n(0) in the post-critical orbit of 0 (namely 0, 4, 196, 1475635396, 4741505474496806234579827160684390596, etc.) must eventually reach 1 (mod p); for a(10) = 503734381, 0 reaches 1 in 7728 steps. Furthermore, the sequence A_n = c_n^2 - 3 (13, 38413, 2177499821928076813, etc.) consists of pairwise coprime integers: gcd(A_i, A_j) = 1 for all i != j.
%C A401173 For generalized maps T_R(x) = (x^2 - 2)^(2^(R-1)) mod p, the universal intersection of unital collapse primes over all R >= 2 is the sequence of Fermat primes A019434 (3, 5, 17, 257, 65537, etc.).
%C A401173 No other terms below 3.5*10^9. By the Cayley-Pruefer-Mertens law, the counting function grows double-logarithmically as O(log(log(X))).
%D A401173 I. M. Zhuk, Distinction-Observation Theory (DOT), Vol. I: Initial Primitives and Arithmetic Dynamics of the Observer, 2026.
%H A401173 Igor M. Zhuk, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23420627">Unital Collapse Primes in Arithmetic Dynamics: Total Backward-Orbit Collapse for T(x) = (x^2 - 2)^2 mod p, Galois Stratification, and Fermat-Totient Asymptotics</a>, Zenodo Preprint (2026).
%F A401173 p is in the sequence iff #{x in {0..p-1} : T^(p)(x) == 1 (mod p)} = p, where T(x) = (x^2 - 2)^2 mod p and T^(k) denotes the k-th iterate of T.
%e A401173 For p = 3: T(0) = 4 == 1 (mod 3), T(1) = 1, T(2) = 1, so all 3 elements of GF(3) reach 1 in 1 step; thus 3 is in the sequence.
%e A401173 For p = 5: T(0) = 4, T(4) = (16-2)^2 = 196 == 1 (mod 5); T(1) = T(4) = 1; T(2) = T(3) = 4 -> 1. All 5 elements of GF(5) reach 1 within 2 steps; thus 5 is in the sequence.
%e A401173 For p = 7: T(1) = T(6) = 1, while 0 -> 4 -> 0 and 2, 3, 5 -> 4 -> 0, so only 2 elements reach 1; thus 7 is not in the sequence.
%p A401173 isColl := proc(p) local s, ns; if not isprime(p) then return false fi; s := {$0..p-1}; do ns := map(x -> ((x^2-2)^2) mod p, s); if ns = s then return evalb(s = {1}) fi; s := ns od end:
%p A401173 select(isColl, [seq(i, i=2..6000)]);
%t A401173 unitalCollapseQ[p_?PrimeQ] := FixedPoint[Union[Mod[(#^2 - 2)^2, p]] &, Range[0, p - 1]] == {1};
%t A401173 Select[Prime[Range[800]], unitalCollapseQ]
%o A401173 (PARI)
%o A401173 isok(p) = {
%o A401173   if (!isprime(p) || p == 2 || (p > 5 && (p%24 == 7 || p%24 == 19 || kronecker(-83, p) != 1)), return(0));
%o A401173   my(st = vector(p)); st[2] = 2;
%o A401173   for (x = 0, p-1,
%o A401173     if (st[x+1] == 2, next);
%o A401173     my(c = x, path = List());
%o A401173     while (st[c+1] == 0, st[c+1] = 1; listput(path, c); c = ((c^2 - 2)^2) % p);
%o A401173     if (st[c+1] == 1, return(0));
%o A401173     for (i = 1, length(path), st[path[i]+1] = 2);
%o A401173   );
%o A401173   return(1);
%o A401173 }
%o A401173 (Python)
%o A401173 from sympy import isprime, legendre_symbol
%o A401173 def is_unital_collapse_prime(p: int) -> bool:
%o A401173     if not isprime(p) or p == 2:
%o A401173         return False
%o A401173     if p > 5 and (p % 24 in (7, 19) or legendre_symbol(-83, p) != 1):
%o A401173         return False
%o A401173     state = bytearray(p)
%o A401173     state[1] = 2
%o A401173     for x in range(p):
%o A401173         if state[x] == 2:
%o A401173             continue
%o A401173         curr, path = x, []
%o A401173         while state[curr] == 0:
%o A401173             state[curr] = 1
%o A401173             path.append(curr)
%o A401173             curr = pow(curr * curr - 2, 2, p)
%o A401173         if state[curr] == 1:
%o A401173             return False
%o A401173         for u in path:
%o A401173             state[u] = 2
%o A401173     return True
%o A401173 print([p for p in range(2, 6000) if is_unital_collapse_prime(p)])
%Y A401173 Cf. A019434 (Fermat primes), A003095 (c_n = c_{n-1}^2 - 2), A000215.
%K A401173 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A401173 1,1
%A A401173 _Igor Zhuk_, Oct 16 2026
